{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"MEASURED: trimming ONLY the rich-text epic's own footprint CANNOT clear this — evidence for 'needs a split or cross-lane coordination' rather than 'the last lane should shave a few lines'.\n\nAttempted, then reverted: condensing both comment blocks the epic added to `_config_schema.py` — `rich_text_cutover` (5 comment lines -> 3) and `dc_pandoc_timeout_s` (7 -> 5), keeping every load-bearing fact (rollback semantics; the 13.5-min / 95.8%-CPU hang the timeout exists for, and why 10s sits between it and the ~0.3s render).\n\nResult: **808 -> 804. Still 4 over.**\n\nGutting the rationale entirely (2 and 3 comment lines) lands at ~801 — STILL over. The two `_SECTION` validator lines the keys require cannot be removed at all. So there is no reachable point where the epic's own lines clear the cap, and shipping degraded comments that fix nothing is strictly worse than leaving them intact. Working tree restored; no partial trim committed.\n\nThis sharpens the near-miss point: `a147945aae` landing at EXACTLY 800 was not merely unlucky, it was unrecoverable-by-that-lane. Once the file sits at the cap, the next key from ANY lane breaks `main`, and the lane that breaks it usually cannot fix it either — the only moves left are a split or an administrator override. That is why a warning band on the module-size gate is worth more here than any after-the-fact trim."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786653187502686001,"uuid":"c7739de5-caf5-4493-94b5-4dac68ac04eb"}